#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](images/10/10c3a1128f78cd19b14ca29bda23bcd3c14a49a6bbf2608aa568f6f7c22aea9b.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](images/83/837556eba2375812c7d20c59533fc51f91716c6306254d2fc193b98315dd5663.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/20/20ee95979c2ae9a6f3eca54e65923f608ef569141c6ff1393ef53e99636dda1a.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/13/13f61ba37464f828258713594c973c4ab7c5c1d6aa50c2b482dcc2b444013686.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](images/69/69506c47b7fce7fd91eee135299db2920598325d4eb28611d758aeb5e3815489.svg)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5505" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/3e/3e68233c9221816c1bd834d2260a162ec6d4cc5e063022c8b9e545948f703589.svg)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](images/43/432aa48b4a3573c076a617dacc461cd5816c5279e79f9f14545609b0b4553f0c.svg)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/7c/7c7e458176039ee582b023ad53137c6adf5d66fdb7e17e2a62aedde6a160545a.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124343 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5506" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/95/95456befbc775262e58e48b5e32503b70e1a675d5c4b79911bfd3d526880d42e.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/9a/9ac30b66a0fb5232b248e7ccf6d5b57267168e328387f233dfdb793fbb7ea287.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/db/dbd8e786b6f8ffa50a35abc0136871f855f56fd4afff75c4ef6542dfa9b08f10.svg)\n\nFinally, ![](images/df/df31aa59467f4b7c37d2406d5ad6571a69baa6f9a7868f6bd868e45cf10c15aa.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7330" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0acea11970e0a11fd3d6df09c46335ca747b58d82e6cf5a3c1bdfe941ccae242.svg) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/00/001911806161a251ef55b16759d586e6d6747a8d34bae00c07eb89c8179cde84.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/41/413d4dbc71e9f4059fc86d85806e009d5d41dfd8a87b9ea159badcc6003602e5.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/18/187539cd8867c5b8592cf3b6f928992f7be756c25a9a641741ba98150072f307.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/a0/a09b2916af73b1d593ac237e5e895ff3d0c31d4978b66e4358dbd5f132547324.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} {:answer/id #uuid "4210454b-1b57-5202-b24c-d9ba774f094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269863} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa01c8c-fa5e-5db3-9890-98afc410a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270630} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b21af7-648d-570d-aab4-7958bcae5273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270762} {:answer/id #uuid "fb277ce2-2359-57d8-9955-89059a164bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98d67e2-0ccf-58ae-aa10-005055ae0213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8196086-ee7b-5323-b641-b0ce439cc41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140262" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c63519-b99a-535f-a767-aba13aa8c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458623} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea786b-6aeb-5ab2-a668-e87a4f9adbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458680} {:answer/id #uuid "279f798e-fca6-5883-b6f2-651083c8d7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458707} {:answer/id #uuid "72c2755d-f02b-5787-8c1c-3f4bebe47eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458827} {:answer/id #uuid "e75890bc-ed87-53a8-8ce4-3a04a435feb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279f798e-fca6-5883-b6f2-651083c8d7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12f6dde-e5b0-5b0d-8795-17186369244e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "381f7568-7449-53f3-9fba-a89117d8269e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37235" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}23 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -43 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 22\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 50 & -41 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 30 & -27 & 24 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{6}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{21}{5} & \\frac{42}{5} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & -19 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$  Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ \nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=1+1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c86e3f43-49a4-5b7f-9408-e35c02f28b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395860} {:answer/id #uuid "e9db2e1f-0ee4-54d1-ae6f-0983601a839e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395861} {:answer/id #uuid "fe222dc3-726c-55f2-a5d5-184f99572360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395862} {:answer/id #uuid "ad98892c-f9b4-525c-8046-a410518643b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395863} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbfc55b-9986-5204-a717-051bef9ec970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86e3f43-49a4-5b7f-9408-e35c02f28b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395860} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2a91b4-7ef6-5580-9c3e-14e555eca865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b036f0f-ab37-53c9-a8c1-aa9fe52bf1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37250" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe1b46a-c909-56fb-a2d0-1c237f2299b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321271} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6e06cb-6b0a-5fad-bef9-74d23b2a7ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321595} {:answer/id #uuid "9635ba7d-da50-51b0-8bfe-6b95edeb0aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322341} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c04ad6-a8f5-5c5d-868a-192ece2ce131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322674} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e57c05-94c4-5c43-a6f5-d08f26fcbdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4e57c05-94c4-5c43-a6f5-d08f26fcbdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322677} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0846fd-32f3-58f4-ac5a-546d5ec6d403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7fb554-d5d8-5ae2-835a-1ad3df46f8dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37839" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+5) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b039043-81b4-5fc5-8708-7fbf237a4ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382067} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdae987-e461-5f61-b5d9-45cafc99eb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382069} {:answer/id #uuid "972e4d42-5535-532c-a9ea-1b24b7a4b9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382070} {:answer/id #uuid "99def5b0-3eb9-5fe1-a95a-3aba50761daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6099f13f-2d83-545c-81d5-a7792b6fc7be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be37aea9-9ebb-51c3-9045-5fceee548409" :question/math-academy-id "q-37843" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed43da4-d269-5604-bbf9-1927b6064761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321263} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa61980-79b8-527e-8940-19201d84a989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321649} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5b8b25-6b89-5520-8175-ac513d31536f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322118} {:answer/id #uuid "cd80fd12-d065-517c-b43c-0e669252996d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322747} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5a8b76-4004-5320-a562-a342de6f52f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa61980-79b8-527e-8940-19201d84a989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321649} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf20a7c-62cb-5309-9c10-149c24b1804e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "479c6325-498e-5e9a-912b-f302956edc64" :question/math-academy-id "q-37845" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.  \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f3e2af-1d0c-5ede-94d8-b2f1171885f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395873} {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba3c2a-7e4a-5f75-b416-64d7e2c0bb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395875} {:answer/id #uuid "842abd30-146b-5529-8174-62765bece360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395876} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e2d4db-4322-5304-a102-6991fb8129e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ff45fc-3165-5e0a-8ffa-750ac771413e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21c29510-ffed-5744-82ad-55435a6f5d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-37846" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4836fd5e-81cb-5822-88e3-f48f8157403d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321717} {:answer/id #uuid "4c88d2c4-061d-5ff8-b3a9-e680dc24529d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321749} {:answer/id #uuid "4d413f86-be6d-5b3e-94b3-b2713b851ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321757} {:answer/id #uuid "aec73220-8ce4-58e7-8226-1a70b406ae45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322520} {:answer/id #uuid "e749c2df-33b1-57ad-80f1-9e82f8b89025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4836fd5e-81cb-5822-88e3-f48f8157403d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321717} :answer-field/id #uuid "22acc19e-c1a6-590b-9eba-de6e85126b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b47eadc-7593-50fb-a74a-94fb681ad288" :question/math-academy-id "q-37849" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a & 0 \\\\ 2 & b & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $2a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=14,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -40 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a-b=2\\cdot 1-0=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be5b898-bb9e-5591-b2be-40a8826536c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322266} {:answer/id #uuid "96425773-76f0-5f4d-9cef-766adcc46216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322342} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f88135-ad46-5a24-8bc8-029eba1b400d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322424} {:answer/id #uuid "ad04d0bc-9bc7-5808-8856-6462aa0f3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322504} {:answer/id #uuid "c73509d6-73cb-5d05-8a65-f7a2276440f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73509d6-73cb-5d05-8a65-f7a2276440f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322692} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88e030a-2a2c-5bfb-8d1f-081203401946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11a60940-c504-5d41-97ac-a5508ab8072e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37850" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & -4 \\\\ -1 & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of  the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-40{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018cb1d9-4080-55b4-8ebf-21d4e8fa4d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458617} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb1276-e1a7-5941-90c5-9145274ba4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458627} {:answer/id #uuid "0d33fde4-e2b1-57a6-8569-020539ed6f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458644} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2adafb-9e4e-5d36-8429-0b4d174295dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458964} {:answer/id #uuid "f370cf1f-96a5-56f0-a5d3-d4f31b71ac03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "018cb1d9-4080-55b4-8ebf-21d4e8fa4d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458617} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db82ef2-8d0e-58c9-8bc4-7c52dc1e4dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7183ce0d-1871-5ef9-9ae5-46b683d591c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83705" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d59528b-cdd3-5b86-8192-072c8f8d7878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348563} {:answer/id #uuid "62e390e1-3e96-5e93-a534-8cd460604753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349352} {:answer/id #uuid "d001947d-99b0-5aaf-8f74-6e8d15704c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350278} {:answer/id #uuid "da87e45a-8c4a-5a42-8899-d6dbd59835c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350366} {:answer/id #uuid "e9666494-f169-5eb6-833c-17b3c4d922d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d001947d-99b0-5aaf-8f74-6e8d15704c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350278} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b01fdd-181f-5f00-abe0-c2498b967af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d8038b9-0f9e-5c31-b69c-95d4cfd71254" :question/math-academy-id "q-83708" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8e2916-1175-544e-9d6d-7e8a9cd2eca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404275} {:answer/id #uuid "812a013d-6c97-5c8d-8555-41c27f26371f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404276} {:answer/id #uuid "62777a5a-2c5b-5c2c-bc49-88fd1f72656d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404277} {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404278} {:answer/id #uuid "1184eb62-ac2c-5e6b-abd1-f1c4d51d39b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404278} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8de3def-44e0-5291-9c5d-3c0a5037be6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c42e4814-ec50-5416-8161-bbb51b0ed62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83710" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd25d24-ca14-582c-9c9a-901bf68be589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458873} {:answer/id #uuid "999a115a-08e8-5499-8e87-7c47414d8221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458895} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7091e-e210-5867-884f-ccfde76bb619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458923} {:answer/id #uuid "aabbbcda-6626-588e-9857-4b315ea6b330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458935} {:answer/id #uuid "e52bd489-6e03-5706-aa17-622a610be235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bd25d24-ca14-582c-9c9a-901bf68be589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458873} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a726a9-a1c8-58dd-882f-1cff70681cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "800b324e-1ca8-5722-9959-a0bda670cec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83847" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c8360c-3a50-5dde-b1bc-982c3982cc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404282} {:answer/id #uuid "f6699882-8461-5d24-9c25-bf00717bab1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404283} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} {:answer/id #uuid "77ed7e08-d0f9-52c5-bb87-4330aef31eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404285} {:answer/id #uuid "4129fe51-c35a-5376-82e4-222db49da083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e147ba3d-c970-55a9-ac96-8187378e9cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82133f4-cf40-52df-89fc-b9095a2ed393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404310} {:answer/id #uuid "243d60de-8290-5d22-a02c-f2dd575ecea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404311} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404312} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be6a6c-ff71-53df-b6a3-b84c12d5df62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404313} {:answer/id #uuid "896a5cf1-06e8-57d7-a9d3-ce00f8cd43ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404312} :answer-field/id #uuid "8345e678-341d-5cbb-a53f-7923d2b2c4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca9903e3-49ec-5aad-b531-9a0f3deac3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84592" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d914700-a622-51bd-b23f-e0c8b5876a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458784} {:answer/id #uuid "e099e7fa-99c6-5f58-b356-c698463df652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459016} {:answer/id #uuid "e137d699-f455-56d7-bea8-ae26fac32af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459018} {:answer/id #uuid "ecac03ad-87df-5528-9573-767c2d3397a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459046} {:answer/id #uuid "f3087984-1725-52d5-aeac-9cfabdeb1733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecac03ad-87df-5528-9573-767c2d3397a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459046} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bafffe9-ad74-503f-8d92-47a0678e49c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af5b5ba4-a618-5f57-bc44-d42484f14c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-84594" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b3f1e3-8c79-5c21-af24-2422b0553cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399217} {:answer/id #uuid "597ee6d4-7ae6-5b66-8819-c98a309ce41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399218} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f71d5e-7c62-59ce-b3eb-ece84bf182c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399219} {:answer/id #uuid "267b3288-c2ab-531e-a4ef-c630d0c9728b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399220} {:answer/id #uuid "2edfc2e7-c27b-566a-a56c-76809ee37046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "597ee6d4-7ae6-5b66-8819-c98a309ce41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399218} :answer-field/id #uuid "453d5361-60be-58a9-a449-bd8996b7f09f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6f6a680-345d-5a5b-99e4-e6fe2833a541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382554} {:answer/id #uuid "2301fa40-f8b0-5fd4-bead-c2e76b13105b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382555} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d79bb0-e5bb-501a-99df-c84429ac1d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382556} {:answer/id #uuid "636ebdcc-8cbb-5b72-9dac-70406d71db0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382557} {:answer/id #uuid "0b566b63-8a34-5b22-a917-fad95ed9297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b566b63-8a34-5b22-a917-fad95ed9297a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382558} :answer-field/id #uuid "db6e6cfe-224b-582d-a339-40b97c0e9678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a6d9730-0724-557b-bd0c-58990ff98311" :question/math-academy-id "q-84604" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-8,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=1-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18548cc4-5579-5860-af3c-2ffb7473c802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -28 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 12\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458669} {:answer/id #uuid "5d535dfb-87df-5251-bb7b-1b059f15fcc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 12\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458783} {:answer/id #uuid "72fd09c1-79de-56af-8b54-d3b0e35dbc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458829} {:answer/id #uuid "87bb53d7-fc61-5729-a383-3a8cc7ab4af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458867} {:answer/id #uuid "900bcbf4-f190-5da3-839e-ba5e69558818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fd09c1-79de-56af-8b54-d3b0e35dbc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458829} :answer-field/id #uuid "e015e886-af0c-50c8-80c7-dbd3c9879cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c742c85e-0648-5cd9-8c31-bb35257f03e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-84609" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following. \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector  ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector  ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -28 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -28 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} {:answer/id #uuid "259df9a5-e148-52b1-8c76-e5f3797156f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351913} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0b1af0-e162-51c8-a699-561daf399c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352507} {:answer/id #uuid "90343dd9-95c9-5f79-b6c6-c97b7b8b1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352816} {:answer/id #uuid "aeab5e76-18bb-5fe4-8750-1b5fd4ac3465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b7eafb-592d-5378-aed9-4f494837b137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7c3932-3369-5860-a2cf-645dd0b0d0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-84611" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
